---
// ═══ S-home — the title block, the peek strip, and the crop the DS cannot make ═
//
// TWO ARTEFACTS FROM ONE FILE: S-home (populated) and R-crop-picker (phone).
// The other six states declare `coverage: "inherits"` and point at the
// treatments plans 12 and 13 sketched on their most demanding hosts.
//
// ═══ THE STATE AXIS IS A ROUTE PARAM. `?state=` DOES NOT WORK ══════════════
// Under `output: 'static'` Astro strips the query string in DEV as well as at
// build (astro/dist/core/request.js:20 — `if (isPrerendered) url.search = "";`),
// which plan 12 proved by getting seven byte-identical bodies from seven
// `?state=` URLs. UI-SPEC is wrong on this point and plan 13's corrected idiom
// is followed instead: ONE FILE PER SCREEN at
// src/pages/admin/<screen>/[...state].astro exporting getStaticPaths, SCREEN and
// STATES. This plan's own frontmatter names `src/pages/admin/home.astro`; that
// path would have produced a screen with no state axis at all, so the idiom
// wins and the deviation is recorded in the SUMMARY.
//
// ═══ WHY THIS ROUTE EMITS EIGHT PATHS, NOT SEVEN ═══════════════════════════
// `phone` is D-09's LAYOUT variant, not a point in the draft lifecycle. It gets
// a getStaticPaths entry and no STATES row, exactly as plan 13 did for
// `filtered-empty`, because adding it to CANONICAL_STATES would silently make
// the coverage table 7 x 7 = 49 cells while every check that counts non-blank
// cells kept passing. The plan suggests reaching the refusal with a
// `?layout=phone` query variant; that mechanism does not exist here for the
// same reason `?state=` does not, so it is a route segment instead.
//
// ═══ THE DATA IS REAL AND WAS COUNTED, NOT QUOTED ══════════════════════════
// Counted out of data/home_config.json this session: 6 peekIds, ONE
// peekPositions entry, 3 socialLinks, 2 ctas. And the measurement that makes
// D-23's argument concrete rather than rhetorical — the six peek photos were
// looked up in portfolio_images.json and measured:
//
//     architecture-singapore          2000x1333   3:2      no crop stored
//     abstract-intothemist            2000x1333   3:2      no crop stored
//     nature-acrossthetrees           2000x1333   3:2      no crop stored
//     architecture-hawamahaldaytime   2000x3000   2:3      "50% 25%"
//     wildlife-kingfisher             2000x1333   3:2      no crop stored
//     architecture-eiffeljpg          2000x1333   3:2      no crop stored
//
// FIVE OF SIX ARE ALREADY THE FRAME'S OWN RATIO, so `object-fit: cover` shows
// essentially all of them and no crop value would change anything. The one
// photo that carries a crop is the one photo that is PORTRAIT in a landscape
// frame. The feature exists for the outlier — which is exactly why a preset
// grid is the wrong shape: it would offer nine choices on the five photos where
// every choice is identical, and fail to express the one value that matters.
//
// ═══ ONE HYDRATION DIRECTIVE, ON ONE LINE ══════════════════════════════════
// This route spends sanctioned island 2 of 3. The acceptance grep counts LINES
// carrying the directive prefix and requires exactly one, so the prefix appears
// nowhere else in this file, not even in prose. `Sortable` for the peek order is
// rendered WITHOUT hydration through SortableStatic — reordering itself is
// demonstrated running, and keyboard-driven, on /admin/photos.

import {
	AlertBanner,
	Badge,
	Button,
	Card,
	Chip,
	Divider,
	EmptyState,
	Eyebrow,
	Field,
	Heading,
	Select,
	Skeleton,
	Text,
	TextInput,
	Textarea,
} from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import Admin from "../../../layouts/Admin.astro";
import FocalPointSketch from "../../../components/FocalPointSketch";
import SortableStatic from "../../../components/SortableStatic";
import { CANONICAL_STATES } from "../../../lib/artefacts.mjs";
import fixtures from "../../../fixtures/home.json";

export const SCREEN = {
	id: "home",
	route: "/admin/home",
	entity: "home_config.json",
	layouts: ["desktop", "phone"],
	proves:
		"That six peek slots plus a focal position fit ONE full-width column with no split pane (D-07), and that the missing crop picker (G-1) is a blocker rather than a nicety. The argument is measured rather than asserted: five of the six peek photos are already 3:2, so a crop changes nothing on them, and the single stored value 50% 25% belongs to the one photo that is 2000x3000 in a 3:2 frame. 25 is not a stop on the nine-point preset lattice, so a preset picker would have had to change the only crop the site has. It also carries R-crop-picker, D-09's designed refusal, at comfortable density on its own route.",
};

// Eight paths from one file. `state: undefined` produces the base path, so the
// populated state is `/admin/home/` and `dist/admin/home/index.html` exists,
// which is what the artefact contract and the acceptance criteria both name.
export function getStaticPaths() {
	return [
		{ params: { state: undefined } },
		...CANONICAL_STATES.map((s) => ({ params: { state: s } })),
		{ params: { state: "phone" } },
	];
}

export const STATES = [
	{ state: "populated", coverage: "designed", ref: "S-home" },
	// INHERITS, not designed. home_config.json always carries a title, a
	// subtitle and an intro — there is no empty Home screen. The one thing on it
	// that can genuinely be empty is a SECTION: the peek strip with zero slots.
	// That is precisely what E-resume-section proves ("an empty section inside an
	// otherwise populated screen"), and it is designed on /admin/resume in the
	// same plan, so the pointer resolves. Inventing an E-home would put a second
	// design in the review for one decision and take the E- set past the five
	// ADMIN-IA enumerates.
	{ state: "empty", coverage: "inherits", ref: "E-resume-section" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 13 claimed T-loading-list on /admin/photos, where the list
	// is 39 rows. Six slots is not a more demanding host.
	{ state: "loading", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-loading-list" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 13 claimed T-error-inline on /admin/photos on the argument
	// that an inline warning is hard to find in a 39-row list and unmissable on a
	// short form. This screen is the short form, so it renders an INSTANCE of the
	// treatment rather than a second design of it.
	{ state: "error", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-error-inline" },
	// INHERITS. Plan 12 designed the badge triple on the dashboard.
	{ state: "dirty", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-dirty-badge" },
	// INHERITS. D-16's per-file diff has ZERO design-system coverage (G-7) and is
	// its own screen.
	{ state: "conflict", coverage: "inherits", ref: "O-conflict-diff" },
	// INHERITS. The publish outcome is one treatment sketched once.
	{ state: "success", coverage: "inherits", ref: "T-success-published" },
];

// THE READ THE STATE AXIS DEPENDS ON. Replacing it with a constant is the
// negative control for check-states.mjs: all seven bodies become identical and
// the gate fails naming this screen.
const state = Astro.params.state ?? "populated";
const fx = fixtures[state];
if (!fx) {
	throw new Error(
		`admin/home/[...state]: no fixture for state "${state}". getStaticPaths emits ` +
			"`undefined`, CANONICAL_STATES and `phone`, and src/fixtures/home.json must carry a " +
			"key for each. A state declared in STATES with no fixture is a fake artefact: the " +
			"route would answer 200, render a plausible page, and be screenshotted under a state " +
			"name it is not in.",
	);
}

// Field takes `wiring` from the useField HOOK, and an Astro page cannot call a
// hook. FieldWiring is a plain data interface, so the object is built by hand —
// plan 13's idiom, unchanged. Ids are unique across the page.
const wiring = (id) => ({
	controlId: id,
	hintId: undefined,
	errorId: undefined,
	describedBy: undefined,
	invalid: false,
});

const noop = () => {};

// UI-SPEC's contract table, verbatim, held in named constants so the exact
// strings are greppable in one place and cannot drift as the markup moves.
const REFUSAL_TITLE = "Crop needs a bigger screen.";
const REFUSAL_DESC =
	"Setting a focal point means dragging inside a 3:2 frame, which doesn't work on a phone. Everything else on this screen does.";
const INLINE_ERROR = "Needs a title before this can be published.";
const SECTION_EMPTY_TITLE = "No photos in the peek strip.";
const SECTION_EMPTY_DESC =
	"Add one, or leave the strip out — Act 1 renders no gallery row when it is empty.";

// The legacy icon set for a social link, recovered from SOCIAL_ICONS in
// legacy:PropertiesPanel.tsx. Three are in use; the select offers all of them.
const SOCIAL_ICONS = [
	{ value: "github", label: "GitHub" },
	{ value: "linkedin", label: "LinkedIn" },
	{ value: "twitter", label: "Twitter" },
	{ value: "instagram", label: "Instagram" },
	{ value: "mail", label: "Email" },
];

// Verbatim from legacy:PropertiesPanel.tsx's homeCta branch — the option LABELS
// carry the parenthetical, and dropping it would lose the only place the admin
// says what the two styles look like.
const CTA_STYLES = [
	{ value: "primary", label: "Primary (filled)" },
	{ value: "secondary", label: "Secondary (outlined)" },
];

const isPhone = fx.layout === "phone";
const isInherit = ["dirty", "conflict", "success"].includes(state);
const showPeek = !["empty", "loading"].includes(state);
const showFocal = ["populated", "error", "dirty", "conflict", "success"].includes(state);

// The one photo that carries a crop, pinned BY ID so the focal frame cannot
// drift onto a photo whose value is a default. Null everywhere the control has
// nothing to do — the island renders unconditionally and returns null there,
// because check-no-js.sh fails an allowlisted route that ships zero script tags.
const focalSlot = showFocal ? (fx.slots.find((s) => s.id === fx.focalId) ?? null) : null;

const peekItems = fx.slots.map((s, i) => ({
	id: s.id,
	index: `${i + 1}`,
	primary: s.title,
	secondary: `Gallery Photo ${i + 1}`,
	thumb: s.src,
	meta: s.position ?? "50% 50% (default)",
	badge: s.position ? { label: "cropped", tone: "info" } : null,
}));

const cropped = fx.slots.filter((s) => s.position).length;
const alreadyFramed = fx.slots.filter((s) => s.orientation === "wide").length;

const reviewStates = ["populated", ...CANONICAL_STATES, "phone"];
const hrefFor = (s) => (s === "populated" ? "/admin/home/" : `/admin/home/${s}/`);
---

<Admin
	title={`S-home / ${state}`}
	active="home"
	route="/admin/home"
	navStates={fx.nav}
	pipeline={fx.pipeline}
	density={fx.density}
>
	<div class="hm-head" data-artefact={state === "populated" ? "S-home" : undefined}>
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">HOME</Eyebrow>
		<Heading level={2} size="lg" weight="bold">{fx.headline}</Heading>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">{fx.sub}</Text>
	</div>

	{/* ── R-crop-picker ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	    D-09's DESIGNED REFUSAL, reached at its own URL. Copy is verbatim from
	    UI-SPEC's contract table and the last sentence is the part that does the
	    work: without "Everything else on this screen does." the panel reads as a
	    broken control rather than as a decision, which is threat T-00-35.

	    THE REFUSAL IS TRUE, NOT DECORATIVE. The legacy control is mouse-only —
	    one mouse-down handler, no pointer and no touch events
	    (legacy:PropertiesPanel.tsx:739-786) — so on a phone it does not respond
	    at all. This route renders the whole rest of the screen underneath, at
	    COMFORTABLE density per UI-SPEC's viewport contract, so the claim in the
	    last sentence is something a reviewer can check rather than take on
	    trust.

	    IN PRODUCTION this is a media query, not a URL: the refusal fires on a
	    coarse pointer at phone width. The sketch gives it a route because plan
	    16 reviews URLs and plan 17 screenshots them at 390x844. */}
	{isPhone && (
		<div class="hm-panel" data-artefact="R-crop-picker">
			<EmptyState title={REFUSAL_TITLE} description={REFUSAL_DESC} />
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				Rendered at comfortable density, not compact: compact drops a text field to 30px, and
				five of the six device classes in the responsive contract are coarse pointer with a
				44px floor.
			</Text>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── The title block. D-07's one full-width column, no split pane ───────
	    The legacy panel titles become section headings and the legacy FIELD
	    labels are kept verbatim underneath them, which is why the first field is
	    "Name" rather than "Site Title": the panel was titled Site Title and the
	    input inside it was labelled Name (legacy:PropertiesPanel.tsx:669-684).
	    ADMIN-IA records two label drifts of this kind and this is a third.

	    Field supplies the label; TextInput does not. Both have a `label` prop and
	    using both renders it twice. defaultValue, never value — a static sketch
	    has no change handler and React's controlled-input warning is not
	    something to ship into a reviewer's console. */}
	<section class="hm-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">SITE TITLE</Eyebrow>
		<Field
			label="Name"
			wiring={wiring("f-home-title")}
			hint="Act 1's headline. It is also the browser tab title."
			errorMessage={fx.titleError ? INLINE_ERROR : undefined}
		>
			<TextInput id="f-home-title" defaultValue={fx.title} error={fx.titleError} />
		</Field>

		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">TAGLINE</Eyebrow>
		<Field label="Subtitle" wiring={wiring("f-home-subtitle")}>
			<TextInput id="f-home-subtitle" defaultValue={fx.subtitle} />
		</Field>

		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">INTRO TEXT</Eyebrow>
		<Field label="Introduction" wiring={wiring("f-home-intro")}>
			<Textarea id="f-home-intro" rows={3} defaultValue={fx.intro} />
		</Field>
	</section>

	{/* ── T-error-inline, INHERITED and instantiated ────────────────────────
	    D-18's lenient half: the message is about PUBLISHING, saving still works,
	    and it attaches to the field it is about. The treatment is plan 13's, so
	    this is an instance rather than a second design — but the instance has to
	    exist, because "saving still works" is invisible unless the save control
	    is on screen and demonstrably enabled.

	    AND THE COMPONENT CANNOT SAY WHICH SEVERITY THIS IS. UI-SPEC specifies
	    "Field + FieldError at warning severity". FieldErrorProps is
	    `{ message, className }` — there is no severity, no tone, and the markup
	    is a fixed `role="alert"` span in --red-ink. D-18's two severities render
	    identically and both interrupt. Reported in the SUMMARY, not worked
	    around: recolouring it locally would hide the gap. */}
	{state === "error" && (
		<div class="hm-panel" data-artefact="T-error-inline">
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">INHERITS T-error-inline</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				The treatment is plan 13's, sketched on the 39-row list where an inline warning is
				hardest to find. What is specific to Home is that the incomplete field is the SITE
				TITLE — the one string every public page renders — so the draft is still saveable and
				the site simply keeps the published title until it is filled in.
			</Text>
			<div class="hm-actions">
				<Button variant="primary">Mark as ready</Button>
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					Save is enabled. This is a warning about publishing, not a rejected edit.
				</Text>
			</div>
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── The peek strip. Six slots, one crop ───────────────────────────────
	    Sortable, rendered WITHOUT hydration: the reorder interaction is already
	    demonstrated running and keyboard-driven on /admin/photos, and the whole
	    budget is spent on the two controls that exist to file findings. */}
	<section class="hm-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">GALLERY PEEK / ORDER</Eyebrow>

		{state === "loading" ? (
			<div class="hm-skeleton" data-artefact="T-loading-list">
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					Waiting for the peek strip. The shell, the title block and Publish already work.
				</Text>
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Skeleton shape="text" />
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					INHERITS T-loading-list. Six Skeleton lines rather than the component's own loading
					state, because Sortable has no loading prop — the one place on this screen where the
					rule "use the component's loading prop, never a bespoke skeleton" has nothing to
					reach for.
				</Text>
			</div>
		) : null}

		{state === "empty" && (
			<div class="hm-panel" data-artefact="E-resume-section">
				<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">INHERITS E-resume-section</Eyebrow>
				<EmptyState title={SECTION_EMPTY_TITLE} description={SECTION_EMPTY_DESC}>
					<Button variant="secondary">Add Photo to Gallery</Button>
				</EmptyState>
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					The design is the résumé screen's, because this is the same fact: a section that is
					empty inside a screen that is not. What is specific to Home is that the title block
					above is still complete and still publishable, so the empty strip is a choice rather
					than an unfinished screen.
				</Text>
			</div>
		)}

		{showPeek && (
			<>
				<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
					{fx.slots.length} slots. {cropped} carries a stored focal position; the other{" "}
					{fx.slots.length - cropped} fall back to <code class="hm-key">50% 50%</code>.{" "}
					{alreadyFramed} of {fx.slots.length} are already 3:2, so a crop would change nothing
					on them.
				</Text>
				<SortableStatic items={peekItems} listId="peek-order" className="hm-peek" />
				<div class="hm-actions">
					<Button variant="secondary">Add Photo to Gallery</Button>
					<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
						Reordering is static here. It runs, keyboard-driven, on /admin/photos — the two
						screens share the component and the budget is one island per route.
					</Text>
				</div>
			</>
		)}
	</section>

	{/* ── Gallery Photo N: the focal control ────────────────────────────────
	    The legacy panel is titled "Gallery Photo {N}" and the field inside it
	    "Position (drag to adjust)", both kept verbatim. "Replace with" is the
	    legacy sub-heading over a photo picker; the picker itself is a Modal +
	    Card grid per ADMIN-IA and is out of this plan's scope, so the heading
	    names the affordance and the button below it is inert.

	    THE ISLAND IS UNCONDITIONAL. It receives `slot={null}` on the states
	    where the control has nothing to do and returns null there. Astro still
	    emits the island element and the hydration script, so the budget gate
	    keeps measuring something real on all eight routes. */}
	<section class="hm-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">GALLERY PHOTO 4 / POSITION (DRAG TO ADJUST)</Eyebrow>
		{showFocal ? (
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				G-1: the design system has no crop or focal-point component, so the control below is
				composed from primitives. It fixes two of the legacy control's three defects — it is
				pointer-aware rather than mouse-only, and the keyboard reaches the value — and cleans
				up its listeners, which the legacy control never did.
			</Text>
		) : (
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				No slot is selected in this state, so the crop control has nothing to point at.
			</Text>
		)}
		<FocalPointSketch client:load slot={focalSlot} frameRatioW={3} frameRatioH={2} />
		{showFocal && (
			<div class="hm-actions">
				<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">REPLACE WITH</Eyebrow>
				<Button variant="secondary">Choose another photo</Button>
			</div>
		)}
	</section>

	{/* ── Social Links: three rows, three fields each ───────────────────────
	    Field + Select needs `ariaLabel` as well, duplicating the visible label.
	    Field renders `<label for>` with no id of its own and Select's trigger is
	    a role="combobox" button, which is not a labelable element — so the label
	    points at nothing and there is no id to feed ariaLabelledBy. Plan 13
	    recorded this as F-13-7; it is composed as prescribed rather than
	    dropped. */}
	<section class="hm-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">SOCIAL LINKS</Eyebrow>
		<div class="hm-rows">
			{fx.socialLinks.map((link, i) => (
				<Card surface="subtle" padding="md" radius="lg">
					<div class="hm-row">
						<Field label="Icon" wiring={wiring(`f-social-${i}-icon`)}>
							<Select
								value={link.icon}
								onChange={noop}
								options={SOCIAL_ICONS}
								ariaLabel="Icon"
								searchable={false}
								size="sm"
							/>
						</Field>
						<Field label="Label" wiring={wiring(`f-social-${i}-label`)}>
							<TextInput id={`f-social-${i}-label`} defaultValue={link.label} />
						</Field>
						<Field label="URL" wiring={wiring(`f-social-${i}-url`)}>
							<TextInput id={`f-social-${i}-url`} defaultValue={link.url} placeholder="https://..." />
						</Field>
					</div>
				</Card>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ── Button: the two CTAs ──────────────────────────────────────────────
	    The legacy panel is titled "Button" and its three fields are Text, Link
	    and Style. ADMIN-IA's recovered list calls the second "Link URL"; the
	    rendered source calls it "Link", and ADMIN-IA already records that drift.
	    The rendered label wins. */}
	<section class="hm-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">BUTTON</Eyebrow>
		<div class="hm-rows">
			{fx.ctas.map((cta, i) => (
				<Card surface="subtle" padding="md" radius="lg">
					<div class="hm-row">
						<Field label="Text" wiring={wiring(`f-cta-${i}-text`)}>
							<TextInput id={`f-cta-${i}-text`} defaultValue={cta.text} />
						</Field>
						<Field label="Link" wiring={wiring(`f-cta-${i}-link`)}>
							<TextInput id={`f-cta-${i}-link`} defaultValue={cta.link} placeholder="/photography" />
						</Field>
						<Field label="Style" wiring={wiring(`f-cta-${i}-style`)}>
							<Select
								value={cta.style}
								onChange={noop}
								options={CTA_STYLES}
								ariaLabel="Style"
								searchable={false}
								size="sm"
							/>
						</Field>
					</div>
					<div class="hm-cta-preview">
						<Chip>{cta.text}</Chip>
						<code class="hm-key">{cta.style}</code>
					</div>
				</Card>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>

	{/* ── dirty, conflict and success: the pointer, not a new treatment ─────
	    All three are declared `inherits` above. Plan 12 sketched them on the
	    dashboard, their most demanding host, and inventing a home-local variant
	    would put two designs in the review for one decision. What is rendered is
	    the coverage decision plus the one thing that is genuinely Home-specific,
	    so a reviewer walking the coverage table can check the `inherits` cell
	    without opening a second document. */}
	{isInherit && (
		<div
			class="hm-inherit"
			data-artefact={state === "dirty" ? "T-dirty-badge" : state === "conflict" ? "O-conflict-diff" : "T-success-published"}
		>
			<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">
				{state === "dirty"
					? "INHERITS T-dirty-badge"
					: state === "conflict"
						? "INHERITS O-conflict-diff"
						: "INHERITS T-success-published"}
			</Eyebrow>
			<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
				{state === "dirty"
					? "The badge is plan 12's. What is specific to Home is that a crop is an edit: nudging the focal point by one percent makes home_config.json pending, and the change list has to name the photo and the value or the operator cannot tell an intentional nudge from a lost one."
					: state === "conflict"
						? "The diff is plan 12's pointer and the design system has no diff component at all. What is specific to Home is that home_config.json is the smallest file in the set — a conflict here is two or three lines, so the per-file diff must read well at tiny sizes as well as at the photo file's length."
						: "The outcome is plan 12's. What is specific to Home is that nothing on this screen is asynchronous: unlike Photos, a published Home is fully live as soon as the build finishes, so the copy must not borrow the photo pipeline's hedging."}
			</Text>
			{state === "dirty" && (
				<div class="hm-badge-row">
					<Badge tone="pending" dot>Draft</Badge>
					<Text size="sm" tone="muted">Home, in the sidebar, in the topbar count, and here.</Text>
				</div>
			)}
			{state === "conflict" && (
				<AlertBanner
					open
					tone="warning"
					title="home_config.json changed after your draft started."
					description="Your peek order and the remote intro text are both newer than the base. Resolving is per file; Photos and Résumé stay pending."
					dismissible={false}
				/>
			)}
		</div>
	)}

	{/* ── Review band. NOT part of the design. ─────────────────────────────
	    State names only, never a state's marker copy: check-states.mjs asserts
	    each marker appears on its own page and no other, and a legend carrying
	    every state's copy would make that assertion pass vacuously. */}
	<aside class="hm-review">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">REVIEW CHROME / NOT PART OF THE DESIGN</Eyebrow>
		<p class="hm-review-line">
			{reviewStates.map((s) => (
				<a class="hm-review-link" href={hrefFor(s)} data-on={s === state ? "true" : undefined}>{s}</a>
			))}
		</p>
		<p class="hm-review-note">
			`phone` is a layout, not a state: it has a route so the refusal can be reviewed and
			screenshotted, and no row in STATES.
		</p>
	</aside>
</Admin>

